\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Static escrow vs.\ competitive Vickrey auction. Both regimes underwrite the same coverage $B_T = \mu (1+s)$; only the financing mechanism differs. In (a) the principal ties up $B_T$ for the coverage period, paying an opportunity cost $r \cdot B_T$. In (b) insurers compete; the principal pays only the second-lowest bid $q^*$. The competitive regime Pareto-dominates the static one under the welfare assumptions of \S  \ref {sec:youle:welfare} below.}}{27}{figure.caption.46}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:auction-inline}{{3}{27}{Static escrow vs.\ competitive Vickrey auction. Both regimes underwrite the same coverage $B_T = \mu (1+s)$; only the financing mechanism differs. In (a) the principal ties up $B_T$ for the coverage period, paying an opportunity cost $r \cdot B_T$. In (b) insurers compete; the principal pays only the second-lowest bid $q^*$. The competitive regime Pareto-dominates the static one under the welfare assumptions of \S \ref {sec:youle:welfare} below}{figure.caption.46}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Pareto dominance of competitive auction over static escrow. Left: dominance rate as a function of reputation noise $\sigma _r$ at $n=5$ insurers, split by cartel size. Dominance holds robustly with no cartel even at $\sigma _r = 0.5$; with 3 colluders it collapses past $\sigma _r \approx 0.1$. Right: dominance rate vs.\ $n$ at $\sigma _r = 0$ and no cartel; $n=3$ is the empirical optimum due to the winner's-curse order-statistic effect.}}{27}{figure.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pareto-mc-inline}{{4}{27}{Pareto dominance of competitive auction over static escrow. Left: dominance rate as a function of reputation noise $\sigma _r$ at $n=5$ insurers, split by cartel size. Dominance holds robustly with no cartel even at $\sigma _r = 0.5$; with 3 colluders it collapses past $\sigma _r \approx 0.1$. Right: dominance rate vs.\ $n$ at $\sigma _r = 0$ and no cartel; $n=3$ is the empirical optimum due to the winner's-curse order-statistic effect}{figure.caption.47}{}}
